WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.750
This Episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare, home to over 20,000

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.759
classes that could teach you a new lift skill.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:10.210
The Ford Model T was not the first car.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.679
Not even close.

00:00:11.679 --> 00:00:15.990
Depending on how you define what a car is,
that honour was achieved 100 years before

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:22.800
Henry Ford was even born in 1769, when French
engineer Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot created this

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.000
steam powered vehicle designed to travel off
rail.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.640
The honour for the first true automobile goes
to Karl Benz, founder of Mercedes Benz, in

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:32.640
1885.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.980
With this single piston 2 stroke gasoline
powered vehicle.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.840
The art of the automobile was well under development
before Henry Ford hit the scene.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.660
No Ford did not invent the automobile, he
invented something much more profound.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.050
He created modern society.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.490
That is a massive claim, but bear with me.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.260
His manufacturing techniques did not just
revolutionise how we design and build everything.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.050
Making complicated machinery like tractors
and cars affordable for the masses.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.620
His manufacturing techniques radically changed
the trajectories of billions of people’s

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.620
careers.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.830
At the turn of the 18th century craft manufacturing
was the status quo.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.329
Defined by a highly skilled workforce.

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:16.999
People wishing to pursue a career in automotive
manufacturing, entered their career and progressed

00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:18.380
through an apprenticeship.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.740
Picking up a huge variety of skills, gradually
learning the tricks of the trade and being

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.709
masters of their craft.

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:27.209
Many would go on to run their own machine
shops.

00:01:27.209 --> 00:01:29.539
These were less employees, and more contractors.

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:34.460
In these days, a machine like an automobile
was not built entirely in house.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.490
Parts would come from smaller machine shops
from all over a city.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.600
They used general purpose tools and machines
to create the parts needed, which would be

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.569
sent to the final assembler.

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:48.969
These part would vary massively from one batch
to another, requiring a skill assembling team.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:52.719
Workers needed to understand the function
of the part they were working on, and skillfully

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:55.619
manipulate the parts together into the final
vehicle.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:59.420
There was no mass manufacturing of complex
machines like this.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.850
Each vehicle was one of a kind, commissioned
by whoever was wealthy enough to afford it.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.529
At this production volume no company could
create a monopoly.

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:12.180
There were hundreds of small craft shops like
this across Western Europe and North America,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.950
but many would soon be run out of business
by Henry Ford before they had the chance to

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.640
adopt his mass manufacturing techniques.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.269
Only the best craft manufacturers survived.

00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:25.640
Companies like Aston Martin and Bentley succeeded
by focusing on the ultra wealthy that could

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.810
afford these one off vehicles, using skilled
craftsmen to build unique and luxurious cars,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.000
but even they would soon have to join the
movement to survive.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.980
All eventually being bought out by these mass
production power houses, no longer able to

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.439
keep up with cost of innovation and manufacturing
required to keep pace in the automotive industry.

00:02:43.439 --> 00:02:48.870
At the high point of the Model T’s success
in 1923, Ford was manufacturing 2.1 million

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:53.610
Model Ts a year, a figure that would only
be matched by a single vehicle model again

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.489
with the VW Beetle.

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:59.310
Many people chalk Henry Ford’s success down
to inflexibility in design.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.700
The famous quote of “You can have colour
as long as it is black”.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.420
This was true for many years, but perhaps
not for the reason you think.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.980
You see, Henry Ford was obsessed with manufacturing
speeds.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.260
The painting process he used allowed the paint
to dry quickly, and it was only available

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.379
in black.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:21.160
[6] He shaved time off wherever possible to
achieve that monumental milestone of 2.1 million

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.700
Model Ts a year.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.200
The Model-T was no fluke, it was the culmination
in over 20 design iterations over 5 years.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.010
Each one tweaking the design and manufacturing
procedure to cut seconds off the total process,

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:37.459
and the innovations continued through the
nearly 20 years of production, that would

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:46.829
see a total of 15 million Model Ts manufactured.

00:03:46.829 --> 00:03:57.780
On the first day of production in 1908, the
average task cycle for the Model T lasted

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.870
514 minutes.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.930
The task cycle time is the length of time
before a single task is repeated.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:08.689
So, the average worker did not repeat a task
for 8 and a half hours.

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:13.530
For Ford, this was essentially how quickly
a single production line was producing vehicles,

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:18.090
as assembly lines cannot start a new vehicle
until another has exited at the other end.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:23.280
So, he set to work on reducing that cycle
time, and by 1913 he managed to bring it down

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.530
to just 2.3 minutes.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.730
For a product this complex, consisting of
hundreds of parts, with hundreds of processes,

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:34.170
that is astounding and was something no other
company had ever achieved.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:38.210
How on earth did Ford achieve this quantum
leap forward in manufacturing speedl?

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:42.690
Let’s first start with innovations that
Ford was not responsible for that allowed

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.540
him to begin this journey.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.860
As I said, one of the reasons highly skilled
workers were essential to these industries

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:53.510
before Ford came along, was because of the
high variability between parts.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.880
In engineering we call this tolerancing.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:00.380
When I worked as a design engineer, I needed
to specify the tolerances I needed for specific

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:01.850
part features.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:06.910
Say I needed a shaft to fit a particular hole,
I need to specify how much the machinist was

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.120
permitted to deviate from the listed dimension.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.090
If I have a 20 mm hole paired with a 19 mm
shaft, and I specify that both can deviate

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.570
from that dimension by plus or minus 0.5 millimeters.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:25.130
Even at the extreme ends of both, where both
are 19.5 mm wide, they will still fit with

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.680
some force.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:31.490
This may not be acceptable depending on application,
and higher tolerancing may be needed, which

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.560
generally means an increase in cost.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.680
Engineers regularly screw up with these things
even today, but in Ford’s day consistently

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:43.930
achieving a tolerance that tight in mass manufacturing
would have been huge task, and was generally

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:49.000
something saved for military applications,
and not for low cost consumer products.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.460
This was largely due to the manufacturing
techniques of the day, specifically heat treatment

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.460
methods.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.750
As explained in my knife forging and aluminium
videos, metals need to be heated and cooled

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:03.970
in specific ways to strengthen or harden the
metal, but this also makes the metal much

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:09.340
harder to cut and shape, so the metals were
often cut first and heat treated after.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:14.330
This heating and cooling causes the metal
to deform due to thermal expansion, which

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.180
can then throw the original piece out of tolerance.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:22.610
This is called warping, and it made it nigh
on impossible to get a consistent final product.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.050
Many attribute Ford with revolutionizing the
standardisation of parts [3], but in truth

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:31.950
he was simply at the right place at the right
time to benefit from technologies that facilitated

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:32.950
it.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:37.000
New methods for cutting and stamping pre-hardened
metals allowed Ford to eliminate much of this

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.650
variability due to warping.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:43.140
Advancements in precision measurement and
manufacturing allowed Ford to be confident

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:47.990
that parts would be interchangeable, and in
turn this allowed Ford to design his vehicles

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.310
in a way that reduced costs.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:54.580
This was the dawn of destruction for the craft
manufacturing industry, and the beginning

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:58.430
of a movement that would change the face of
modern society.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.660
While his competitors were casting each cylinder
of their engine blocks separately and bolting

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:07.520
them together, due to the difficulty in casting
a single part with multiple holes that needed

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.050
to line up precisely.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:13.440
Ford casted a single complex engine block,
that drastically reduced the time required

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.900
to manufacture and assembly it.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:20.530
This of course, led to incredibly expensive
dedicated machinery needed to manufacture

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.500
a single piece of the vehicle.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.110
In the world of craft manufacturing, a skilled
worker could use a general purpose tool and

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.680
skillfully use it to produce the final product.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.900
In the world of mass manufacturing this was
not acceptable.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:37.460
It took too long and required skilled workers
who were too difficult to replace.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:42.460
For example, engine blocks consist of an upper
and lower part that need to mate perfectly

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.730
to maintain a seal for engine compression.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:49.690
Ford’s competitors, like Cadillac, used
a single flexible milling machine to create

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:54.050
a flush surface on both the upper and lower
halves of the engine block.

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:59.080
Engine blocks and heads were loaded and milled
slowly and precisely one at a time.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:04.060
Ford instead created dedicated machines to
mill engine blocks and engine heads separately,

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.030
15 and 30 at a time respectively.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:11.360
Workers simply snapped the unmilled pieces
into a tray while the previous lot was being

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.810
milled, and then pushed the tray into place
when the time came.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:18.040
A worker could be trained in 5 minutes to
do this task.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:21.550
They didn’t need to speak the same language
as the person next to them.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.080
They didn’t need to think about anything
else.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.270
Just feed the machine.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:28.120
Like the little butter passing robot of Rick
and Morty,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.800
“What is my purpose?

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.160
You pass butter.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:36.020
OH MY GOD.”
these people had a single purpose.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.590
This of course resulted in inflexibility in
design.

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:44.450
The cost of introducing an entirely new model
vehicle was drastically increased.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:49.360
This is why, even today, that car brands tend
to iterate on old designs rather than introduce

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.920
entirely new models.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:56.260
It’s simply too expensive and time consuming
to retrofit entire production lines.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.440
When Ford eventually decided to completely
redesign the Model T and produce the Model

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.690
A, these machines were thrown out, but he
had achieved his goal, cycle times were lowering,

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:08.110
and there was still room to improve.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:14.210
Ford managed to half cycle times from 2.3
minutes to 1.2 minutes, with his next innovation.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:18.550
When production first started on the Model
T employees worked from a stationary work

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:19.550
stand.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:23.340
If they needed a part or tool they would get
up and get it themselves.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:28.650
Ford soon recognized the waste and introduced
dedicated stock suppliers, who’s only job

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:32.820
was to ensure that other workers had the parts
they needed to keep production running at

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:34.500
a constant pace.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.420
Where possible these employees were replaced
with automated supply lines.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:43.700
This idea grew and evolved to the introduction
of Ford’s greatest manufacturing innovation,

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:48.870
the moving assembly line, a manufacturing
technique still in use today, even for huge

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:50.550
machines like planes.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:56.500
This is Boeing’s moving assembly like for
the 777, which carries the entire 166 tonne

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:01.760
plane across the factory ford, gradually adding
the 3 million parts needed to complete it.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.570
This method introduces a sense of urgency
to the factory floor, an ever looming deadline

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.610
to complete your work before the plane reaches
the next production step.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:14.530
If there is a critical problem the entire
production line will literally stop moving

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:15.710
until it’s fixed.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.490
[5] Ford was not the first to introduce such
an idea.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:22.880
They had been used in simple production lines
for butchering of carcasses and food preparation

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:28.270
before, but never for anything this complex
and Ford applied it as a science.

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:33.090
Using his engineering skills to help pioneer
a new branch of engineering, industrial engineering.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:38.590
A branch of engineering mostly concerned with
optimizing the logistics of manufacturing.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:42.810
Spend a few hours playing Factorio and you
will really gain an appreciation for the complexity

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:43.880
of this profession.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.279
It starts off simple, but as your factory
grows you need to carefully manage production

00:10:48.279 --> 00:10:50.370
speeds to avoid pile ups.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:54.370
You need to plan and place your production
units to maximize transportation speeds, and

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:56.460
get the products where they need to be.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:01.240
One of the huge problems you run into is faster
production units producing items faster than

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:05.730
you need them further up the line, causing
pile ups of stock that can back up and actually

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:10.690
block other production units, before you know
it production has grinder to a halt.

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:15.090
The moving assembly line forces all workers
to work at the same pace.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:19.430
Faster workers can’t produce items faster
than they are needed, and slower workers can’t

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:20.590
slack off.

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:25.040
Ford’s innovations catapulted The Ford Motor
Company to international success, but that

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.410
dominance would not last forever.

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:30.730
Companies came from all over the world to
observe Ford’s factories.

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:35.720
They marveled at how the factory itself was
like a finely tuned machine, each part feeding

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.030
into the other.

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:41.990
They took their lessons home, and by 1955
mass manufacturing had proliferated around

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:45.840
the world, and soon companies outside the
US were not just catching up with the big

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.130
three of Detroit.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:51.930
Ford, General Motors and Cadillac, but far
exceeding their capabilities.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:57.190
Most notably Toyota in post world war 2 Japan
who led a revolution in manufacturing through

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.820
a new manufacturing philosophy, lean manufacturing.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:06.100
This graph shows all vehicles produced by
region after 1955, showing the explosion in

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:10.800
growth in Japan with the advent of lean manufacturing,
that would lead to Detroit’s eventually

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:11.839
demise.

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:14.150
Detroit would soon become a ghost town.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:18.000
Just as these companies viewed their machines
as disposable, they viewed their employees

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.360
as disposable.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.790
Dropping them the moment demand tanked.

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:23.790
This problem has only got worse.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:27.490
Zero hour contracts and strict control of
unions is common.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:31.500
Ford actually paid his workers incredibly
well for the time, but that meant little when

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:36.150
demand dropped and these workers had few transferable
skills to gain new employment.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:41.230
For better or worse Ford’s innovations completely
changed the job market for billions of people.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:45.310
I was one of those people, working 12 hour
shifts with only one task.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.410
Weigh a stent and pass it to the next step.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:52.180
During that time I made extra efforts to learn
new skills outside of work to keep myself

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.180
sane.

